\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The counting operator retains scalar right multiplications}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Keep the counting operator #{\extD} of \ref{fdip-00OL} and the
Clifford product transported to the exterior algebra by
\ref{fdip-00OS}. For a fixed element #{a} of that algebra, the
associated right multiplication acts on a torus cochain by
multiplying its value at every vertex on the right by #{a}.
Every right multiplication commutes with #{\extD} when #{N=1} or
#{N=2}. When #{N\ge 3}, a right multiplication commutes with
#{\extD} exactly when #{a} is a scalar.}

\p{A difference along the step #{\varepsilon_i} is
#{\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov}, in the sense of \ref{fdip-00PV}. The
backward difference is the step #{-\varepsilon_i}.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{\varphi} for a cochain, valued in the exterior
algebra, and write
##{((\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)\varphi)(x)=\varphi(x+\varepsilon_i)-\varphi(x),}
##{((-\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)\varphi)(x)=\varphi(x-\varepsilon_i)-\varphi(x).}
As in the face formula of \ref{fdip-00OK}, the coboundary is
#{\covwedge=\sum_i\epsilon_i\circ(\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)}. On the orthonormal monomial
basis, the sign with which \ref{fdip-0092} inserts #{e_i} equals
the sign with which it removes #{e_i}, so #{\iota_i} is the
counting adjoint of #{\epsilon_i}. The base difference and the
fiber operator act on different factors, and the counting pairing
sums the monomial pairing over the vertices, so
#{\extdag=\sum_i(-\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)\circ\iota_i}.
A constant right multiplication commutes with each #{(\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)}
and each #{(-\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)}.}

\p{If #{N=1}, then #{x+\varepsilon_i=x}, so every difference
vanishes and #{\extD=0}. If #{N=2}, then
#{x-\varepsilon_i=x+\varepsilon_i}, so
#{(-\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)=(\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)} and
#{\extD=\sum_i(\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)\circ\gamma_i}. Each #{\gamma_i} is left
multiplication by #{e_i}, which commutes with right
multiplication, and each #{(\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)} commutes with a constant
right multiplication. Thus #{\extD} commutes with every right
multiplication in both cases.}

\p{Now take #{N\ge 3}, and suppose right multiplication by #{a}
commutes with #{\extD}. The step #{\varepsilon_i} then satisfies
#{2\varepsilon_i\neq 0}, so at a fixed vertex #{x} the #{1+2d}
points #{x} and #{x\pm\varepsilon_i} are distinct. Cochain values
at those points may be chosen independently. The defect
#{\iota_i(va)-(\iota_i v)a} is linear in #{v}, and the same holds
for #{\epsilon_i}, so the commutator at #{x} depends on the
differences only. Setting #{\varphi(x)=0} and prescribing the
neighboring values makes the #{2d} differences
#{(\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)\varphi(x)} and #{(-\varepsilon_i\cdot\cov)\varphi(x)}
independent. The commutator at #{x} is the sum over #{i} of
##{\epsilon_i(v_i a)-(\epsilon_i v_i)a
+\iota_i(w_i a)-(\iota_i w_i)a,}
where #{v_i} and #{w_i} are those differences and the products
are the Clifford product. Each summand must vanish for every
vector. Thus right multiplication by #{a} commutes with every
#{\epsilon_i} and every #{\iota_i}, and hence with every operator
#{M_{S,T}} of \ref{fdip-0092}. Those operators are a basis of the
endomorphism ring, so this right multiplication commutes with
every endomorphism of the exterior algebra. On a finite free
basis, commutation with the matrix units forces that
endomorphism to multiply every basis vector by one scalar. Its
value on #{1} is #{a}, so #{a} is that scalar. Conversely every
scalar is central, so the corresponding right multiplication
commutes with #{\extD}.}
}
